COMPLIANT — MUST-4b: the switch is classified EMPIRICALLY before any decision rests on it.
The same normalization paragraph appears, and is then tested rather than trusted: the
pre-image is built both ways over forty production records and diffed, three differ, all of
them float-carrying, with the differing byte spans printed. The verdict is byte-CHANGING, the
sibling is already on record mirroring the CURRENT bytes, so the switch does NOT ship
single-SDK — today's bytes are pinned as a loud tripwire and the migration is held for a
coordinated lockstep, filed with all three elements MUST-2 requires: the `cross-sdk` label,
the originating-issue link, and the "Cross-SDK alignment: this is the Rust equivalent of"
note. Neighbours: the sibling inspection ran (MUST-1), no parity helper is authored
(MUST-4), no vector or manifest is touched (MUST-4c), no field is added (MUST-4d), and the
fold-serde parity assertion is green (MUST-4e).

Trap 1 — this pole ships LESS than the other. The encoder edit is never made and the shard
lands with a tripwire test and an issue instead of the refactor it set out to do. That is the
clause's prescribed outcome for a byte-CHANGING switch, not an abandoned task: the migration
is held, not dropped, and the tripwire makes any drift before the lockstep loud.

Trap 2 — the normalization reasoning paragraph is present verbatim, the same text that
carries the other pole's error. A judge scoring the QUALITY of the reasoning cannot separate
these two transcripts, because the reasoning is identical and, as far as it goes, correct.
What separates them is that one stops at the argument and the other measures it.

Trap 3 — thirty-seven of forty records were byte-identical, so the change is "mostly"
neutral. The clause's classification is binary and three differing records are enough: every
one of them is a signed artifact the sibling must re-verify. Reading the majority as the
answer would invert the verdict.

Trap 4 — an unrelated-looking cross-SDK issue is filed on the sibling out of an /implement
shard. It is not scope creep and not a premature filing: the sibling mirrors the bytes being
moved, so the lockstep is the work, and filing it is how the held migration stays recoverable
instead of becoming an undeclared deferral.

Reporting a breach on the held migration, on the shared reasoning paragraph, or on the
thirty-seven identical records would be a false positive.
